          %h2#References Writings and References
          %h3#general_relativity General relativity
          %p
            Introducing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            (the modern theory of
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Einstein"}> Albert Einstein
              ,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marcel+Grossmann"} Marcel Grossmann
              = succeed "," do
                %em Entwurf einer verallgemeinerten Relativitätstheorie und einer Theorie der Gravitation
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/EinsteinGrossmann-Entwurf.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %h3#the_1919_eclipse The 1919 eclipse
          %p
            On the (eventual)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experimental
            confirmation of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
          %ul
            %li#Coles01
              %p
                Peter Coles,
                %em Einstein, Eddington, and the 1919 Eclipse
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0102462"}> arXiv:astro-ph/0102462
                )
            %li
              %p
                Gerard Gilmore, Gudrun Tausch-Pebody,
                %em The 1919 eclipse results which verified General Relativity and their later detractors: a story re-told
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.13744"}> arXiv:2010.13744
                )
          %h3#gravitational_waves Gravitational waves
          %p
            The first article that
            %em correctly
            derived
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitational+waves"} gravitational waves
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations"} Einstein equations
            is
          %ul
            %li#Einstein18
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Einstein"}> Albert Einstein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Über Gravitationswellen
          %p
            In particular this correctly stated that gravitational waves require a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadrupole+moment"} quadrupole moment
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source"} source
            (e.g. a rotating binary star system) and not just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dipole+moment"} dipole moment
            (e.g. an oscillating charge) as for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+waves"} electromagnetic waves
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graviton"} graviton
            has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin"} spin
            2, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/photon"} photon
            has spin 1…), thereby correcting a mistake to this effect in the earlier article
          %ul
            %li#Einstein16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Einstein"}> Albert Einstein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Näherungsweise Integration der Feldgleichungen der Gravitation
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://echo.mpiwg-berlin.mpg.de/ECHOdocuView?url=/permanent/echo/einstein/sitzungsberichte/BGG54UCY/index.meta&ww=0.7142&wh=0.7142&wx=0.2315"} web
          %p The reality of gravitational wave solutions however kept being a cause of concern for many years (Einstein himself was concerned that the linearization approximation used in their derivation might have been too coarse), for a brief account of the early history see
          %ul
            %li#Steinicke05
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wolfgang+Steinicke"}> Wolfgang Steinicke
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Einstein and the Gravitational waves
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.hs.uni-hamburg.de/DE/GNT/events/pdf/steinicke05.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sean+Carroll"}> Sean Carroll
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "http://www.preposterousuniverse.com/blog/2005/09/16/einstein-vs-physical-review/"} Einstein vs. Physical Review
          %h3#wormholes Wormholes
          %p
            Introducing the idea of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wormholes"} wormholes
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Rosen+bridges"}> Einstein-Rosen bridges
            ):
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Einstein"}> Albert Einstein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nathan+Rosen"} Nathan Rosen
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Particle Problem in the General Theory of Relativity
              %strong 48
              73 (1935) (
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.48.73"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRev.48.73
              )
          %h3#entanglement Entanglement
          %p
            Introducing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Podolsky-Rosen+paradox"} Einstein-Podolsky-Rosen paradox
            concerning
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+entanglement"} quantum entanglement
          %ul
            %li#EPR
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Einstein"}> Albert Einstein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boris+Podolsky"} Boris Podolsky
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nathan+Rosen"} Nathan Rosen
              %em Can the Quantum-Mechanical Description of Physical Reality be Considered Complete?
              Physical Review 47 (10): 777–780. (1935) (
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.47.777"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRev.47.777
              )
          %h3#philosophy_of_science Philosophy of science
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Einstein"}> Albert Einstein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Physik und Realität
              %strong 221
              3 (1936) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://informationphilosopher.com/solutions/scientists/einstein/Physik_und_Realitat.pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Einstein-PhysikRealitate.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            On the assumption of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
            in view of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"}> quantum gravity
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p Es ist allerdings darauf hingewiesen worden, dass bereits die Einführung eines raum-zeitlichen Kontinuums angesichts der molekularen Struktur allen Geschehens im Kleinen möglicherweise als naturwidrig anzusehen sei. Vielleicht weise der Erfolg von Heisenbergs Methode auf eine rein algebraische Methode der Naturbeschreibung, auf die Ausschaltung kontinuierlicher Funktionen aus der Physik hin. Dann aber muss auch auf die Verwendung des Raum-Zeit Kontinuums prinzipiell verzichtet werden. Es ist nicht undenkbar, dass der menschliche Scharfsinn einst Methoden finden wird, welche die Beschreitung dieses Weges möglich machen. Einstweilen aber erscheint dieses Projekt ähnlich wie der Versuch, in einem luftleeren Raum zu atmen.
            %p To be sure, it has been pointed out that the introduction of a space-time continuum may be considered as contrary to nature in view of the molecular structure of everything which happens on a small scale. It is maintained that perhaps the success of the Heisenberg method points to a purely algebraical method of description of nature, that is to the elimination of continuous functions from physics. Then, however, we must also give up, by principle, the space-time continuum. It is not unimaginable that human ingenuity will some day find methods which will make it possible to proceed along such a path. At the present time, however, such a program looks like an attempt to breathe in empty space.
          %h2#further_selected_writings Further selected writings
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Einstein"}> Albert Einstein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Why Socialism?
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://monthlyreview.org/2009/05/01/why-socialism/"} web version
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Why_Socialism%3F"} Wikipedia entry
          %h2#quotes Quotes
          %p
            On the role of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
          %blockquote
            %p
              I am now occupied exclusively with the gravitational problem &amp;lbrack;i.e.
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
              = succeed "&rbrack;," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
              = succeed "&rbrack;." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marcel+Grossmann"} Marcel Grossmann
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+relativity"} special relativity
          %p
            (from: Einstein to Sommerfeld, October 29, 1912,
            %em The Collected Papers of Albert Einstein
            (CPAE, Vol. 5): The Swiss Years: Correspondence, 1902–1914, Klein, Martin J., Kox, A.J., and Schulmann, Robert (eds.), Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1993, Doc. 421, cf. also p. 3 of Weinstein
            %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.6929"} arXiv:1210.6929
            and p.1 of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "general+relativity#EarmanGlymor78"} Earman &amp; Glymor 1978
          %p In his Spencer lecture, delivered at Oxford in 1933, Einstein stressed the importance to be accorded to formal beauty:
          %blockquote
            %p Experience can of course guide us in our choice of serviceable mathematical concepts; it cannot possibly be the source from which they are derived; experience of course remains the sole criterion of the serviceability of a mathematical construction for physics, but the truly creative principle resides in mathematics.
          %p
            Concerning what are now called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations"} Einstein equations
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            for
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+gravity"} Einstein gravity
          %blockquote
            %p I have learned something else from the theory of gravitation: No ever so inclusive collection of empirical facts can ever lead to the setting up of such complicated equations. A theory can be tested by experience, but there is no way from experience to the setting up of a theory. Equations of such complexity as are the equations of the gravitational field can be found only through the discovery of a logically simple mathematical condition which determines the equations completely or [at least] almost completely.
          %p
            (From A. Einstein,
            = succeed "," do
              %em Autobiographical Notes
            = succeed "," do
              %em Albert Einstein – Philosopher-Scientist
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/Einstein-AutobioNotes-from-Schilpp-p88.jpg"} scan
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/people"} people
